>
  <p>A marine gearbox should not be identified from brand and approximate size alone. Model, ratio, rotation, serial number, input arrangement and output connection can all affect whether a replacement unit or spare part is suitable.</p>

  <p>Before requesting a marine gearbox quotation, collect the information below from the installed gearbox, its nameplate and the connected engine.</p>

  <h2>1. Photograph the gearbox nameplate</h2>
  <p>Take a sharp, straight photo of the complete nameplate before cleaning, painting or removing anything from the unit.</p>
  <p>Record every relevant field shown, including:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Manufacturer or brand</li>
    <li>Full model/type</li>
    <li>Serial number</li>
    <li>Reduction ratio</li>
    <li>Rated input power or torque if stated</li>
    <li>Rated input speed if stated</li>
    <li>Any order, drawing or specification number</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Confirm the reduction ratio</h2>
  <p>The ratio is a critical gearbox identifier. Do not assume that two gearboxes with the same model have the same ratio.</p>
  <p>Copy the ratio exactly as shown on the plate or documentation. If the plate is damaged, check the vessel manual, gearbox documentation or previous service records before guessing.</p>

  <h2>3. Record input and output rotation information</h2>
  <p>Rotation and installation arrangement matter when evaluating a gearbox replacement. State the direction information exactly as documented by the maker or vessel records.</p>
  <p>If you are not certain how the maker defines the viewing direction, do not translate it into your own “left” or “right” description. Send the original notation and photos instead.</p>

  <h2>4. Identify the connected engine</h2>
  <p>Include the engine maker, full engine model, rated output and rated RPM when known. The existing engine-to-gearbox pairing provides important context for checking a replacement.</p>

  <p>If the gearbox is driven through a flexible coupling or adapter housing, include photos and identification details for those components as well.</p>

  <h2>5. Photograph the input and output connections</h2>
  <p>Useful photos include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Input housing and coupling area</li>
    <li>Output flange</li>
    <li>Mounting feet</li>
    <li>Oil cooler connections</li>
    <li>Control or selector mechanism</li>
    <li>PTO or auxiliary drive if fitted</li>
    <li>Any monitoring sensors or electrical connectors</li>
  </ul>

  <p>When a direct replacement is required, these physical details can be as important as the model name.</p>

  <h2>6. Measure the flange only when documentation is incomplete</h2>
  <p>If identification data is missing, dimensions can support comparison. Useful measurements may include output-flange diameter, pilot diameter, bolt circle, bolt count, shaft size and mounting-foot spacing.</p>

  <p>Measurements should be treated as supporting evidence. A close dimensional match does not prove internal compatibility.</p>

  <h2>7. State the exact scope of supply</h2>
  <p>Clarify whether you need:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Complete marine gearbox</li>
    <li>Clutch or friction components</li>
    <li>Bearings and seals</li>
    <li>Gear set</li>
    <li>Oil pump</li>
    <li>Valve/control components</li>
    <li>Heat exchanger or oil cooler</li>
    <li>Output flange or coupling parts</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If the internal part name is uncertain, provide the gearbox details plus photos of the removed component and its markings.</p>

  <h2>8. Describe the current failure or reason for replacement</h2>
  <p>Useful context includes slipping, delayed engagement, abnormal noise, oil contamination, overheating, leakage, damaged teeth or a requirement to replace a complete damaged unit.</p>

  <p>These symptoms help explain the enquiry but should not be treated as a diagnosis by themselves.</p>

  <h2>9. Use this gearbox enquiry checklist</h2>
  <div class="checklist">
    <p><strong>Gearbox maker:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Model:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Serial number:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Reduction ratio:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Rotation notation:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Engine maker/model:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Engine power/RPM:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Required part or complete gearbox:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Required condition:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Quantity:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Destination:</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Nameplate and connection photos attached:</strong> Yes / No</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Compare model-specific listings carefully</h2>
  <p>Browse the current <a href="/category/marine-gearbox">marine gearbox category</a> for available model references. Examples include the <a href="/products/twin-disc-mg521-marine-reverse-gearbox">Twin Disc MG521 marine reverse gearbox</a> and a <a href="/products/reconditioned-reintjes-marine-gearbox-560kw">reconditioned Reintjes marine gearbox listing</a>.</p>

  <p>Use model listings as a starting point. Final suitability should be checked against the nameplate, ratio, engine application and physical installation details of the gearbox being replaced.</p>
